INT. SPACE ELEVATOR COUNTERWEIGHT STATION - CONTINUOUS

A cylindrical chamber lined with frost-furred cable bundles. Emergency strips pulse amber. Through a cracked viewport, the curve of the Earth drops away, blue and silent.

CAPTAIN IRENE VASQUEZ-LU, 50s, close-cropped silver hair, hangs in a harness over a open maintenance panel. BODE ADEYEMI, 30s, a stocky engineer, floats beside her with a spanner. DR. SANJAY KUMARESAN, 40s, thin, wire glasses, clings to a rail and stares at a handheld readout. TAVITA FONOTI, 22, a rookie in an oversized pressure suit, wedges himself into a corner.

IRENE
Status.

BODE
Tether strain at ninety-four percent. We're twelve minutes from shear.

IRENE
Doctor?

SANJAY
(to the readout)
No. No, that's wrong. That cannot be wrong.

IRENE
Doctor Kumaresan.

SANJAY
The oscillation isn't mechanical. It's rhythmic. It's... answering us.

TAVITA
Answering what?

SANJAY
Our own vibrations. Something on the cable is repeating them back, delayed.

Irene unclips her harness, kicks across the chamber, and grips the rail beside Sanjay. She reads the numbers over his shoulder. Bode drops the spanner, which drifts away unnoticed, and swims to the viewport.

BODE
That's impossible. There's no one out there.

IRENE
Everybody, quiet. Listen.

They go still. The station groans. Under it, faint and regular, three taps. A pause. Three taps.

TAVITA
It's coming from below the panel.

BODE
(low)
It's a cable creak.

IRENE
It's not a creak, Bode.

SANJAY
(muttering)
Hypothesis one, resonance. Hypothesis two, a micrometeorite impact pattern. Hypothesis three...

He trails off. He does not say hypothesis three.

TAVITA
Captain, permission to open the panel?

IRENE
Negative. Nobody touches it. Bode, can we cut the tether remotely?

BODE
We cut it, the whole counterweight goes. Ten thousand tons falls to the ground.

IRENE
Then we don't cut it.

BODE
We don't have a lot of other options.

Three more taps. Louder. The panel rattles in its frame.

TAVITA
It's trying to get in.

SANJAY
It isn't trying to get in. It's trying to get our attention.

IRENE
Then it has it.

She pushes off the rail, drifts to the panel, and presses her palm flat against the metal. The taps stop. A long silence. Bode holds his breath. Sanjay's readout flashes red and then goes blank.

IRENE (CONT'D)
(whispering to herself)
Come on. Talk to me.

From inside the panel, a single tap. Then another, softer, as if from a hand pressing back.

BODE
Captain, don't.

IRENE
Everyone, strap in. We're opening it.

TAVITA
With respect, Captain, no.

IRENE
Strap in, Tavita.

Tavita obeys, fingers fumbling at the clasps. Bode grabs the spanner from the air. Sanjay seals his visor.

SANJAY
(to himself)
Please be a cable. Please be a cable.

Irene fits the spanner to the first bolt. It turns with a groan. The second bolt turns. The panel peels back a hand's width.

A cold blue light spills through the gap. All four faces turn pale in it.

TAVITA
What is that?

IRENE
Hold your positions.

BODE
I'm holding.

SANJAY
Captain, the readout's back. It says we have forty minutes.

IRENE
It said twelve a minute ago.

SANJAY
It says forty now. Something changed the number.

The blue light brightens. The tapping resumes, slow and calm, like a heartbeat.

IRENE
Then we don't have twelve minutes. We have as long as it wants.
